{
  "rules": [
    "Horizon-dependent bias: forecasts are close for days 1–10 but become increasingly too low from ~day 15 onward, especially in rising regimes (systematic underestimation of late-horizon levels). Apply an upward drift correction that increases with lead time (e.g., +0.5 to +1.0 €/t by day 10, +2 to +4 €/t by days 20–30 when trend is up).",
    "Trend/lag effect: the model reacts to upswings with a delay; it captures direction but not magnitude, leading to persistent negative errors during rallies. Use a momentum adjustment: if last 5–10 days return is positive, add a proportional uplift to the whole path (larger for longer horizons).",
    "Spike handling: after sharp upward moves in the realized series, the model mean-reverts too aggressively and misses the continuation (underpredicts post-spike plateau). Add a 'post-spike persistence' rule: if a 1–3 day jump > ~2 €/t occurred recently, reduce mean-reversion strength and shift forecasts upward for the next 2–3 weeks.",
    "Asymmetry: downside phases are forecast more accurately than upside phases; errors are predominantly negative during the Oct rally. Use regime detection (uptrend vs downtrend) and apply corrections mainly in uptrend regimes; keep minimal/no correction in downtrends.",
    "Level anchoring: forecasts appear anchored near ~62–66 even when the market transitions to ~68–70, indicating insufficient level adjustment. Add a level calibration: blend forecast with a rolling realized mean plus recent trend (e.g., 70% model + 30% [last 10d mean + slope*lead])."
  ]
}
